ACTION PLAN OCTOBER 22 2026

1. Implement the delivery-date policy end to end.
Represent estimates as known, explicitly absent, or unknown. Use the latest date in a range, settle the calendar/timezone convention, revalidate immediately before submission, and persist the accepted estimate. Exactly 14 days passes; later dates reject with exact cancellation/readback; ambiguous evidence stops for review.
Completion criterion: checkout decisions and saved order evidence agree across boundary dates, ranges, midnight/DST cases and changed estimates.

2. Complete payment and informational-email ingestion.
Establish real authenticated payment-template evidence, then connect payment processing to the intake workflow. Retain relevant update-email/deal associations without allowing those messages to trigger reservations. Preserve the approved one-reimbursement-per-reservation model and route unsupported payments to review.
Completion criterion: a verified payment records idempotently, updates outstanding exposure, and permits subsequent reconciliation without manual bookkeeping.

3. Validate providers and the native database in parallel.
Collect sanitized, nonmutating evidence first for Gmail provenance, BFMR browser/API alignment, tracker completeness and financial semantics, Amazon checkout/tracking layouts, and BFMR update contracts. Validate the existing schema and installer against disposable native PostgreSQL using the intended non-owning role.
Completion criterion: each contract has dated evidence tied to a code revision, with native constraints, permissions and ownership-loss behavior demonstrated.

4. Reconcile and perform the controlled cutover.
Back up actual SQL and private state, resolve historical creation terms and payment evidence, and review both business and Gmail import reports. Apply only reviewed inputs under separately authorized execution.
Completion criterion: startup accepts the exact account/source hashes, historical records reconcile, and restoration cannot replay provider actions.

5. Package and accept production operation.
Add service supervision, reboot/manual-auth procedures, durable storage configuration, health signals, log retention, coordinated backup/restore, and operator repair tools. Exercise the actual worker through offline integration and failure scenarios, then perform narrowly authorized provider acceptance and a supervised pilot.
Completion criterion: reboot, process crash, lost responses, database failure, expired Gmail history and failed alerts all produce predictable, recoverable behavior.
